# Ignore a few istio.io/istio vulnerabilities. These CVEs are from very old versions of istio for which patches have already been merged - these come up as false positives from trivy because we pin the dependencies and trivy is unable to determine that the pinned versions already have the fix. This is due to istio's tags not following go's strict semver and therefore falling back to a go pseudo version.
CVE-2019-14993
CVE-2021-39155
CVE-2021-39156
CVE-2022-23635

# https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-6m8w-jc87-6cr7
# This CVE only impacts OPA when being run as an exposed standalone server, not when used as a library by extauth,
# or when OPA is used as an unexposed sidecar.
CVE-2025-46569

# https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-pwhc-rpq9-4c8w
# This CVE does not affect the library but the installable
CVE-2024-25621

# https://github.com/solo-io/gloo/issues/11400
# Reported against usr/local/bin/kubectl in the kubectl job image. That binary is copied
# from the upstream rancher/kubectl image rather than built here, so we cannot patch it,
# and no published tag fixes it: the newest, v1.36.2, ships Go 1.26.4, x/net v0.49.0 and
# x/text v0.33.0, all below the fixed versions, and govulncheck reports the same 18
# findings for it as for the v1.35.6 we use. Trivy reports 13 of those 18; they are the
# IDs listed below.
#
# govulncheck confirms the vulnerable symbols are compiled into that binary, so this is a
# containment argument rather than a reachability one. In this image kubectl only runs as
# a short-lived client in the chart's five Helm hook Jobs: resource-rollout,
# resource-rollout-check, resource-rollout-cleanup, resource-cleanup and
# resource-migration. It listens on no port and serves no traffic, so no untrusted party
# can hand it input; its inputs are argument lists fixed in the chart templates, manifests
# the chart renders into a mounted ConfigMap, and responses from the authenticated
# in-cluster API server. Reaching any of these findings therefore requires already
# controlling the API server, cluster DNS, or the operator's own chart values.
#
# Five have no trigger here at all. CVE-2026-56853 (net/http HTTP/2 preface
# ReadHeaderTimeout) and CVE-2026-56862 (crypto/tls post-handshake KeyUpdate flood) need a
# server, which kubectl never runs. CVE-2026-25681, CVE-2026-27136 and CVE-2026-56858 are
# XSS in HTML rendering, and kubectl renders no HTML for anyone. Of the rest,
# CVE-2026-39821 (idna accepting ASCII-only Punycode) and CVE-2026-39822 (os.Root
# following a trailing-slash symlink) only matter if an attacker controls the API server
# hostname or the Job's filesystem, and the remaining six are hangs, panics or stack
# exhaustion on malformed input. The worst case is therefore a hung or crashing hook Job,
# a visibly failed install, upgrade or uninstall, running under ServiceAccounts limited to
# gloo/gateway CRs, the release namespace's Jobs, read access to Deployments and CRDs and,
# for the pre-delete cleanup hook, deleting ValidatingWebhookConfigurations. That is not a
# persistent compromise or an escalation over what such an attacker already holds.
#
# These IDs are ignored repo-wide, and trivy reads this file from the checked-out branch
# for every version the scheduled scan covers. If any is ever reported against a binary we
# build, that is a different exposure and must be fixed rather than covered by this entry.
# Remove once jobs/kubectl/Dockerfile picks up a rancher/kubectl built with Go 1.26.6+,
# x/net v0.56.0+ and x/text v0.39.0+.
CVE-2026-25681
CVE-2026-27136
CVE-2026-33814
CVE-2026-39821
CVE-2026-46600
CVE-2026-56852
CVE-2026-33818
CVE-2026-39822
CVE-2026-56853
CVE-2026-56858
CVE-2026-56859
CVE-2026-56860
CVE-2026-56862
